---
title: Execution context
description: IExecutionContext is a pooled, explicitly-passed parameter — not ambient state. What it carries, and the lifetime rules that come with pooling.
---

`IExecutionContext` travels with a dispatch. It carries the cancellation token, a key/value
bag for that dispatch, and the controls that let a handler or interceptor stop the pipeline.

Two properties define everything else about it:

1. **It is a parameter, never ambient.** Every handler and interceptor receives it
   explicitly. There is no `AsyncLocal`, no thread-local, and no static accessor.
2. **It is pooled.** A dispatch rents a context and returns it on completion. The context is
   valid **only for the duration of that dispatch**.

{% aside type="danger" %}
`AmbientExecutionContext` was **removed in v2**. If you are porting v1 code that reached for
it, see [Migrating from v1](/ergosfare.docs/migration/v1-to-v2). The context parameter is now
the only access path.
{% /aside %}

## What it carries

```csharp
public interface IExecutionContext
{
    CancellationToken CancellationToken { get; }
    IDictionary<object, object?> Items { get; }

    void Set(string key, object item);
    bool Has(string key);
    T Get<T>(string key) where T : notnull;
    bool TryGet<T>(string key, out T item);

    void Abort(object? messageResult = null);
    ExecutionContextScope CreateScope();
}
```

### Cancellation

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
```

The token comes from the mediator call — either the `CancellationToken` argument or, for the
context-taking overloads, inherited from the parent context. Because it arrives on the
context rather than as a separate handler parameter, forgetting to thread it is not possible.

### The item bag

`Set`/`Get`/`Has`/`TryGet` share data along one dispatch. A pre-interceptor can stash
something the handler and post-interceptors read back:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        context.Set("started", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class StopTimer : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ICommand command, object? result, Exception? ex, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

`Get<T>` throws `KeyNotFoundException` if the key is absent and `InvalidCastException` if the
stored value is the wrong type. Use `TryGet<T>` when absence is expected.

You can seed the bag for a dispatch from the mediation settings:

```csharp
var settings = new CommandMediationSettings { Items = { ["tenant"] = tenantId } };
await commands.SendAsync(command, settings);
```

### Aborting

`Abort()` ends the pipeline immediately — no further handlers or interceptors run. If the
message has a result type and you abort before the main handler, you must supply the result:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.CanCreateProducts)
            context.Abort(Guid.Empty);   // result required: the pipeline still owes one

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

`CreateProduct` is an `ICommand<Guid>`, so the pipeline owes the caller a `Guid`. Aborting
before the main handler runs means nothing else will produce one — hence the argument. For a
command with no result, `Abort()` takes nothing.

## Pooling and the lifetime rule

Contexts are rented from a `[ThreadStatic]`-first pool — a plain load and store on the common
same-thread cycle — and returned when the dispatch completes. There is a synchronous fast
path that skips the async state machine entirely when the whole pipeline completes
synchronously.

This is where the ~2.3 MB per 100 000 dispatches comes from. It also imposes one rule:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Never hold onto a context after its dispatch returns.** Once the dispatch completes the
context goes back to the pool and will be handed to an unrelated dispatch. Capturing it in a
field, a closure that outlives the call, or a fire-and-forget task will read another
message's state.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// Wrong — the context is recycled the moment HandleAsync returns.
public ValueTask HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
{
    _ = Task.Run(async () =>
    {
        await Task.Delay(1000);
        context.Set("late", true);      // this context now belongs to someone else
    });
    return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
}
```

If you need something after the dispatch, copy the values out before returning.

## No service provider, on purpose

`IExecutionContext` exposes no `IServiceProvider`. Handler resolution belongs to the
dispatcher, which resolves instances from the calling DI scope. If a handler needs a service,
take it in the constructor — that keeps the dependency visible to the container and to
whoever reads the class.

## Nested dispatch

`CreateScope()` opens a child context for calling the mediator from inside a handler. It gets
its own clean item bag and inherits the parent's cancellation token. That has enough detail
to deserve its own page: [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

Next: [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).
